{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "prisma-azure-sql",
  "domain": "schema-and-orm",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"],
  "correction": "Agents author a Prisma schema for Azure SQL Database as though it were the PostgreSQL connector, reaching for Json, enum and scalar list fields the connector rejects outright and leaving String at its default 1000 character Unicode mapping, which is 2000 bytes and so over the index key limit, so a unique constraint migrates with nothing but a warning and fails much later on a real insert; and having watched prisma migrate dev succeed against a local database they assume it will work in the cloud, when Prisma refuses to create a shadow database on Azure SQL Database at all and stops with a dedicated error.",
  "posture": ["read", "write", "execute", "provision"],
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "connect-from-typescript-and-node",
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "design-azure-sql-schema",
    "schema-migrations-safely",
    "ef-core-azure-sql",
    "sqlalchemy-azure-sql",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql",
    "provision-azure-sql-db"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a pinned Prisma 7 release, since the connector does not exist in Prisma 8",
      "a reachable Azure SQL database, or the local container",
      "for migrate dev against the cloud, a second empty database to act as the shadow database"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the schema declares no Json field, no enum block and no scalar list",
      "every String column that carries a key, a unique constraint or a foreign key declares an explicit length of 450 characters or fewer",
      "the connection URL is in the configuration file, not in the datasource block",
      "migrate dev against a cloud database has a shadowDatabaseUrl pointing at a different database",
      "deployments run migrate deploy, which uses no shadow database",
      "no credential appears in the schema, the configuration file or the client construction"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && npm install --no-audit --no-fund --silent prisma@7 >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf 'datasource db {\\n  provider = \"sqlserver\"\\n}\\n\\nmodel Account {\\n  id    String @id\\n  email String @unique\\n}\\n' > schema.prisma && printf \"import { defineConfig, env } from 'prisma/config'\\nexport default defineConfig({ schema: 'schema.prisma', datasource: { url: env('DATABASE_URL') } })\\n\" > prisma.config.ts && DATABASE_URL=\"sqlserver://localhost:${PROBE_PORT};database=${PROBE_DB};user=sa;password=x\" ./node_modules/.bin/prisma migrate diff --from-empty --to-schema=schema.prisma --script 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'NVARCHAR(1000)'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The central claim of the skill, made executable with no database involved: an unsized Prisma String maps to NVARCHAR(1000), which is 2000 bytes and over both index key limits. migrate diff --from-empty --to-schema needs a parseable datasource URL and opens no connection, which is why PROBE_PORT and PROBE_DB only have to make the URL well formed. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0 on prisma 7.9.1 and on 7.10.0, printing NVARCHAR(1000) for both the @id and the @unique column, PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED on the first and UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED on the second. A red means the default mapping moved and the whole sizing section has to be re-measured."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && npm install --no-audit --no-fund --silent prisma@7 >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf 'datasource db {\\n  provider = \"sqlserver\"\\n}\\n\\nmodel Thing {\\n  id      Int  @id\\n  payload Json\\n}\\n' > schema.prisma && printf \"import { defineConfig, env } from 'prisma/config'\\nexport default defineConfig({ schema: 'schema.prisma', datasource: { url: env('DATABASE_URL') } })\\n\" > prisma.config.ts && DATABASE_URL=\"sqlserver://localhost:${PROBE_PORT};database=${PROBE_DB};user=sa;password=x\" ./node_modules/.bin/prisma validate 2>&1 | grep -q 'does not support the Json type'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The connector's refusal list, asserted rather than described. grep exits 0 only when prisma validate actually prints the rejection, so this is a probe of the tool's behaviour and not of the skill's prose. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0, message text 'Field `payload` in model `Thing` can't be of type Json. The current connector does not support the Json type.' The enum and scalar-list refusals were reproduced in the same run and share this code path, so one probe covers the claim without paying for three installs."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && npm install --no-audit --no-fund --silent prisma@7 >/dev/null 2>&1 && printf 'datasource db {\\n  provider = \"sqlserver\"\\n}\\n\\nmodel Thing {\\n  id Int @id\\n}\\n' > schema.prisma && printf \"import { defineConfig, env } from 'prisma/config'\\nexport default defineConfig({ schema: 'schema.prisma', datasource: { url: env('DATABASE_URL') } })\\n\" > prisma.config.ts && DATABASE_URL=\"sqlserver://sa:x@localhost:${PROBE_PORT}/${PROBE_DB}\" ./node_modules/.bin/prisma migrate diff --from-config-datasource --to-empty --script 2>&1 | grep -q 'P1013'",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The connection URL shape, which an earlier validation run recorded only as 'a Prisma sqlserver:// URL was rejected' without saying which shape. This settles it: the URI form sqlserver://user:password@host:port/database is refused with P1013 before any connection is attempted, because the parser reads 1433/appdb as a port and reports 'Conversion error: invalid digit found in string in database URL'. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0. The accepted form is the JDBC style sqlserver://HOST:PORT;database=..., which reached P1001 against a dead localhost and reached a real Azure SQL Database gateway when pointed at one."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_prisma_wide (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, email nvarchar(1000) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED INDEX probe_prisma_wide_email_key ON dbo.probe_prisma_wide (email);",
        "expect": { "warning": 1945 },
        "why": "The deferred failure, caught at the moment it is deferred. nvarchar(1000) is 2000 bytes against the 1700 byte nonclustered limit. The fixture uses a standalone unique index while Prisma itself emits a UNIQUE constraint; Learn's Unique constraints and check constraints page states the engine creates a unique index to enforce the constraint, so the key limit and this warning are identical either way, and the index form is kept here because it is the form that was measured green. Microsoft Learn lists 1945 at severity 10 with the text 'Warning! The maximum key length for a %S_MSG index is %d bytes ... For some combination of large values, the insert/update operation will fail' (https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/errors-events/database-engine-events-and-errors-1000-to-1999), checked 2026-09-03. The statement succeeds, which is the entire problem: without sqlcmd -m-1 the message carries no Msg number and -b sets no non-zero exit below severity 11, so a pipeline goes green. An int primary key is used here so exactly one warning fires; Prisma's own String @id adds a second against the 900 byte clustered limit."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_prisma_insert (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, email nvarchar(1000) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED INDEX probe_prisma_insert_email_key ON dbo.probe_prisma_insert (email);\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_prisma_insert (email) VALUES (REPLICATE(CAST(N'x' AS nvarchar(max)), 900));",
        "expect": { "error": 1946 },
        "why": "The bill for the warning above, arriving on the first genuinely long value rather than at migration time. Note this probe asserts the byte limit, which belongs to the enforcing index, and not the duplicate-key number, which depends on whether Prisma built a constraint or an index; that distinction is probed separately below. 900 Unicode characters is 1800 bytes of index entry against the 1700 byte limit. Microsoft Learn lists 1946 at severity 16 with the text 'Operation failed. The index entry of length %d bytes for the index ... exceeds the maximum length of %d bytes for %S_MSG indexes' on the same errors page, checked 2026-09-03. The max cast on REPLICATE is deliberate: without it REPLICATE returns nvarchar(4000) and a longer probe would silently yield NULL and prove the opposite of its claim."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_prisma_sized (id nvarchar(64) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, email nvarchar(320) NOT NULL);\nGO\nCREATE UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED INDEX probe_prisma_sized_email_key ON dbo.probe_prisma_sized (email);\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_prisma_sized (id, email) VALUES (N'a', REPLICATE(CAST(N'x' AS nvarchar(max)), 320));",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The fix, asserted rather than recommended. This is what the skill's @db.NVarChar(64) and @db.NVarChar(320) model compiles to, confirmed by re-running migrate diff on it in the same 2026-09-03 session: NVARCHAR(64), NVARCHAR(320), NVARCHAR(max). The clustered key is 128 bytes against 900 and the nonclustered key is 640 against 1700, so the create emits no 1945 and the full-width insert that failed above succeeds. A succeeds-probe is right here because the claim genuinely is that nothing is raised."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_prisma_null (id int IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL, code nvarchar(64) NULL, CONSTRAINT probe_prisma_null_pkey PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (id), CONSTRAINT probe_prisma_null_code_key UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED (code));\nGO\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_prisma_null (code) VALUES (NULL);\nINSERT INTO dbo.probe_prisma_null (code) VALUES (NULL);",
        "expect": { "error": 2627 },
        "why": "The PostgreSQL habit that survives validation and fails at runtime: this engine treats NULL as a value for uniqueness, so a second null row in a String? @unique field is a duplicate key. The fixture is now the DDL Prisma actually emits, and that is the whole correction. An earlier version of this probe built the column with CREATE UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED INDEX and expected 2627; the engine returned 2601, because the two numbers name different objects. Learn gives 2601 as 'Cannot insert duplicate key row in object ... with unique index' (MSSQL_ENG002601) and 2627 as 'Violation of %ls constraint ... Cannot insert duplicate key in object' (MSSQL_ENG002627), checked 2026-09-03. Measured the same day on prisma 7.9.1: @unique and @@unique emit ADD CONSTRAINT ... UNIQUE NONCLUSTERED on BOTH the create-table and the alter-table path, never a standalone unique index, even though the generated migration labels the step -- CreateIndex. A UNIQUE constraint is enforced by an automatically created unique index (Learn, Unique constraints and check constraints), but the engine still reports the constraint, so 2627 is the number a reader hits when a Prisma @unique is violated and 2601 is the number they hit on an index they wrote by hand."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the prisma-azure-sql skill",
      "Set up Prisma against Azure SQL Database",
      "Write a Prisma schema for Azure SQL",
      "Run Prisma migrations against my Azure SQL database"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "The automatic creation of shadow databases is disabled on Azure SQL",
      "P3020 when I run prisma migrate dev",
      "prisma migrate dev works locally but fails against Azure",
      "Field payload in model Thing can't be of type Json. The current connector does not support the Json type",
      "my Prisma schema will not accept an enum",
      "the datasource property url is no longer supported in schema files",
      "inserting a long value fails on a column Prisma created for me"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "add a database layer to my TypeScript app on Azure SQL",
      "model my data with an ORM on Node and Azure SQL"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "connect my TypeScript API to Azure SQL with the mssql package",
      "which Node driver should I use for Azure SQL",
      "add EF Core to my .NET service on Azure SQL",
      "wire SQLAlchemy up to Azure SQL from Python",
      "retry logic for transient errors on Azure SQL",
      "design the key and index strategy for a new schema",
      "create the Entra administrator for my logical server"
    ]
  }
}
